#pragma once
#include <string_view>
namespace atomx::references {
struct Entry {const char *group,*title,*detail,*url;};
inline constexpr Entry entries[]={
    {"interface","Microsoft Windows GDI · Grid-fitted font glyphs","Microsoft Learn, GetGlyphOutlineW and GetGlyphIndicesW. AtomX builds a grayscale font atlas from installed Windows fonts at each actual DPI size, with native grid fitting and compact baseline alignment. Rasterization happens only on font/DPI changes; Dear ImGui/D3D11 draws the atlas. No font files or extra font-library DLLs are redistributed.","https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/wingdi/nf-wingdi-getglyphoutlinew"},
    {"interface","Mozilla Firefox · Tab overflow and tab overview","Mozilla Firefox Help. AtomX borrows bounded tab shrinking followed by horizontal scroll controls, compact pinned tabs, and a searchable all-tabs list; AtomX renders its own title ellipsis and tooltips.","https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/tabs-organize-websites-single-window"},
    {"interface","Microsoft Win32 · Per-monitor DPI and move/size messages","Microsoft Learn, Windows desktop API. AtomX handles WM_DPICHANGED with physical-size fonts and refreshes chrome during the native move/size loop using WM_TIMER; completed GPU scene textures are reused while dragging.","https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/hidpi/wm-dpichanged"},
    {"interface","User supplied AtomX workspace design · v5","AtomX molecular structure editor (1).zip / AtomX 1.0 v5.dc.html, supplied October 2026. Reference for neutral button surfaces, colored tool icons, browser tab context commands, pinning, rename, duplication and reopening, grouped toolbars, compact typography and settings layout. Native Dear ImGui implementation; no numerical methods taken from the template.",""},
    {"lj","OpenKIM Universal LJ · Elliott & Akerson","MO_959249795837_003 (2018 revision of 2015 model); DOI 10.25950/962b4967. Parameters: Angstrom/eV, 4 sigma cutoff, energy shift, Lorentz-Berthelot. Fitted to dimers only. CDDL-1.0; original package and license: third_party/openkim-lj.","https://openkim.org/id/LJ_ElliottAkerson_2015_Universal__MO_959249795837_003"},
    {"lj","LJ equations, mixing and cutoff conventions","LAMMPS official documentation is the equation/convention reference. AtomX implements its own evaluator; LAMMPS is not linked or required.","https://docs.lammps.org/pair_lj.html"},
    {"lj","Lorentz-Berthelot / geometric mixing","LAMMPS pair_modify: arithmetic sigma and geometric epsilon; geometric sigma/epsilon alternative. Energy shifting does not shift forces.","https://docs.lammps.org/pair_modify.html"},
    {"lj","UFF · Rappe et al. (1992)","JACS 114, 10024-10035; DOI 10.1021/ja00051a040. Only LJ parameters are used, not the complete UFF force field. Select types explicitly. sigma=x/2^(1/6), epsilon=D * 0.043364104241800934 eV per kcal/mol.","https://doi.org/10.1021/ja00051a040"},
    {"lj","Imported UFF CSV data origin","User-supplied Desktop CSV was derived from Open Babel data/UFF.prm. That table is imported at runtime, not embedded in AtomX. Open Babel distribution: GPL-2.0; see upstream notices. Import does not perform chemical atom typing.","https://github.com/openbabel/openbabel/blob/master/data/UFF.prm"},
    {"elements","Covalent radii · Cordero et al. (2008)","Covalent radii revisited, Dalton Transactions, 2832-2846; DOI 10.1039/B801115J. Used for displayed element defaults and geometric bond/hydrogen heuristics, not an energy model.","https://doi.org/10.1039/B801115J"},
    {"elements","Van der Waals radii · Bondi (1964)","J. Phys. Chem. 68, 441-451; DOI 10.1021/j100785a001. Undefined entries have explicit fallback display sizes.","https://doi.org/10.1021/j100785a001"},
    {"elements","Jmol / CPK element colors","Reference for element RGB colors in src/elements.hpp. AtomX does not embed the Jmol application.","https://jmol.sourceforge.net/jscolors/"},
    {"symmetry","Spglib 2.7.0","Crystal symmetry, space-group operations, primitive-cell conversion. BSD-3-Clause; license and source: third_party/spglib. Togo et al., Sci. Technol. Adv. Mater. Methods 4 (2024), 2384822.","https://doi.org/10.1080/27660400.2024.2384822"},
    {"symmetry","Spglib conventions","Column lattice vectors in Spglib; row lattice vectors in AtomX. Coordinate/cell conversion follows these definitions.","https://spglib.readthedocs.io/en/stable/definition.html"},
    {"modeling","Materials Studio UI reference","BIOVIA Materials Studio: workflow/UI reference, together with the user's MS UI operation record. AtomX tools are independently implemented; this is not an MS calculation backend.","https://www.3ds.com/products/biovia/materials-studio"},
    {"analysis","OVITO workflow and modifier documentation","Workflow reference for AtomX's independently implemented pipeline/analysis tools. Per-feature audit notes: docs/parity/reference. Algorithm limitations are documented in those notes; this does not claim identical implementation.","https://docs.ovito.org/reference/pipelines/modifiers/index.html"},
    {"analysis","Steinhardt order parameters · Steinhardt, Nelson & Ronchetti (1983)","Phys. Rev. B 28, 784-805; DOI 10.1103/PhysRevB.28.784. AtomX computes the per-particle q4 and q6 from the N nearest neighbors with equal weights; w_l and neighbor-averaged variants are not implemented.","https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.28.784"},
    {"analysis","Bond-angle structure analysis · Ackland & Jones (2006)","Phys. Rev. B 73, 054104; DOI 10.1103/PhysRevB.73.054104. AtomX uses the published cosine bins and cutoffs to classify FCC, HCP, BCC, icosahedral or Other. Verified against ideal crystals only.","https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.73.054104"},
    {"analysis","Atomic strain · Shimizu, Ogata & Li (2007)","Mater. Trans. 48, 2923-2927; DOI 10.2320/matertrans.MJ200769. Source of the least-squares local deformation gradient, Green-Lagrangian strain and von Mises shear invariant. Neighbors are taken from the reference configuration within a cutoff.","https://doi.org/10.2320/matertrans.MJ200769"},
    {"analysis","Non-affine displacement · Falk & Langer (1998)","Phys. Rev. E 57, 7192-7205; DOI 10.1103/PhysRevE.57.7192. AtomX publishes the D2min residual as Nonaffine Squared Displacement, in squared length units, without the neighbor-count normalization some tools apply.","https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevE.57.7192"},
    {"analysis","Wigner-Seitz defect analysis · Nordlund et al. (1998)","Phys. Rev. B 57, 7556-7570; DOI 10.1103/PhysRevB.57.7556. Particles are assigned to the nearest reference site. Vacancies are empty sites; interstitials are extra particles on a site. No affine cell mapping or per-type occupancy.","https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.57.7556"},
    {"analysis","Identify diamond structure · Maras et al. (2016)","Comput. Phys. Commun. 205, 13-21; DOI 10.1016/j.cpc.2016.04.001, Appendix A. AtomX applies the published second-shell common-neighbor test to separate cubic and hexagonal diamond, then labels their first and second neighbor shells.","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cpc.2016.04.001"},
    {"analysis","Structure factor, direct method","Standard definition S(k) = |sum exp(i k.r)|^2 / N, matching the formula in the OVITO manual. AtomX sums the allowed wave vectors of the periodic cell and averages by |k|, with k in inverse length including 2 pi. The Debye method, partial factors and X-ray form factors are not implemented.","https://docs.ovito.org/reference/pipelines/modifiers/structure_factor.html"},
    {"analysis","Shortest-path rings · Franzblau (1991)","Phys. Rev. B 44, 4925-4930; DOI 10.1103/PhysRevB.44.4925. AtomX finds rings of the bond graph in which ring distance equals graph distance. Rings are listed in data tables; no ring polygons are drawn.","https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.44.4925"},
    {"analysis","Diffusion coefficient from the Einstein relation","Windowed mean-squared displacement with a free-intercept linear fit, as described in the OVITO manual. AtomX reports the total 3D value only, with no a/b/c decomposition, selection or drift removal.","https://docs.ovito.org/reference/pipelines/modifiers/diffusion.html"},
    {"analysis","Voronoi tessellation · Rycroft (2009), Voro++","Chaos 19, 041111; DOI 10.1063/1.3215722. AtomX builds each cell by clipping a convex polyhedron with neighbor bisector planes, the cell-based approach Voro++ describes; Voro++ itself is not used or included. Radical (Laguerre) planes follow the standard power-distance weighting. No polyhedra mesh output yet.","https://doi.org/10.1063/1.3215722"},
    {"analysis","Polyhedral template matching · Larsen, Schmidt & Schiøtz (2016)","Modelling Simul. Mater. Sci. Eng. 24, 055007; DOI 10.1088/0965-0393/24/5/055007. AtomX matches FCC, HCP, BCC, ICO, SC, cubic/hexagonal diamond and graphene templates (diamond and graphene by the two-shell extension). Correspondences come from AtomX's own rotation search with bijective assignment, not the paper's convex-hull graph matching, and neighbors are ordered by distance. Default RMSD cutoff 0.1 and default types FCC/HCP/BCC as in OVITO. Ordering types are not computed.","https://doi.org/10.1088/0965-0393/24/5/055007"},
    {"analysis","PTM reference library and OVITO PTM templates","The MIT-licensed PTM library by P. M. Larsen (github.com/pmla/polyhedral-template-matching, master as of 2026-11) defines the RMSD, scale and interatomic-distance formulas AtomX reproduces; none of its code is included. Template coordinates and their scaling follow the OVITO 3.17 PTM reference-template page; orientations are reduced to the cubic, hexagonal or icosahedral fundamental zone by largest |w|.","https://www.ovito.org/docs/current/reference/pipelines/modifiers/polyhedral_template_matching_templates.html"},
    {"analysis","Grain segmentation (OVITO 3.17 workflow)","Workflow, parameters and outputs from the OVITO manual: PTM structure types and orientations as input, Grain IDs 0-based with -1 for no grain, grains table sorted by size with centers and radii from unwrapped coordinates, and the grains-merge sequence. AtomX's edge rules, twin-boundary handling (15 degree tolerance), automatic threshold (robust outlier test on log distance against log merge size) and Dijkstra orphan adoption are its own; thresholds are not calibrated against OVITO.","https://docs.ovito.org/reference/pipelines/modifiers/grain_segmentation.html"},
    {"analysis","Node pair sampling · Bonald, Charpentier, Galland & Hollocou (2018)","Hierarchical graph clustering using node pair sampling, arXiv:1806.01664. AtomX uses the distance d(a,b) = w(a) w(b) / (W w(a,b)) with node weights from summed edge weights, edge weights exp(-theta^2/3) with theta the misorientation in degrees (as stated in the OVITO manual), and nearest-neighbor-chain merging.","https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.01664"},
    {"analysis","Minimum spanning tree grain tracking · Panzarino & Rupert (2014)","JOM 66, 417-428; DOI 10.1007/s11837-013-0831-9. Source of grain identification from misorientation between neighboring crystalline atoms; AtomX's spanning-tree mode merges PTM neighbors in increasing order of misorientation (Kruskal).","https://doi.org/10.1007/s11837-013-0831-9"},
    {"analysis","Orphan atoms · Hoffrogge & Barrales-Mora (2017)","Comput. Mater. Sci. 128, 207-222; DOI 10.1016/j.commatsci.2016.11.027. Definition of orphan atoms (not in any grain) adopted by the nearest grain; AtomX uses the shortest path through nearest-neighbor lists.","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.commatsci.2016.11.027"},
    {"analysis","Elastic strain calculation (OVITO 3.17 workflow)","Parameters (lattice, a0, c/a, push-forward) and outputs (Elastic Strain XX YY ZZ XY XZ YZ, Volumetric Strain = trace/3, Elastic Deformation Gradient, Structure Type 0-5) from the OVITO manual. OVITO derives the ideal lattice from its dislocation-analysis cluster graph; AtomX instead identifies the lattice and its stacking partner with PTM (RMSD cutoff 0.1) and fits each atom's deformation gradient to its own matched template, so values at defects and for hcp atoms in fcc can differ, and no Cluster property is produced.","https://docs.ovito.org/reference/pipelines/modifiers/elastic_strain.html"},
    {"analysis","Chill+ · Nguyen & Molinero (2015)","J. Phys. Chem. B 119, 9369-9376; DOI 10.1021/jp510289t. AtomX correlates the q3 bond order of bonded molecules within the cutoff (default 3.5, as in OVITO), counts eclipsed (-0.35 < c < 0.25) and staggered (c < -0.8) bonds, and classifies particles with exactly four neighbors. Type ids 0-5 follow the OVITO manual.","https://doi.org/10.1021/jp510289t"},
    {"analysis","Local pair entropy · Piaggi & Parrinello (2017)","J. Chem. Phys. 147, 114112; DOI 10.1063/1.4998408. AtomX integrates the Gaussian-smoothed per-atom g(r) by the trapezoid rule on int(cutoff/sigma)+1 points, as in OVITO's published Calculate local entropy script (OVITO 3.17), with its defaults cutoff 5, sigma 0.2. Averaging uses (s_i + sum s_j)/(N_i + 1) from LAMMPS compute entropy/atom. Result in units of k_B.","https://doi.org/10.1063/1.4998408"},
    {"analysis","Adaptive common neighbor analysis · Stukowski (2012)","Modelling Simul. Mater. Sci. Eng. 20, 045021; DOI 10.1088/0965-0393/20/4/045021. Used by Identify fcc planar faults to find hcp-like atoms from the 12 nearest neighbors with the local cutoff (1+sqrt 2)/2 times their mean distance.","https://doi.org/10.1088/0965-0393/20/4/045021"},
    {"analysis","Identify fcc planar faults (OVITO 3.8+ workflow)","Workflow and type ids from the OVITO manual: non-hcp, indeterminate, intrinsic stacking fault, twin boundary, multi-layer fault, with areas from the basal area per atom divided by 2 (ISF) or 1 (twin). OVITO builds on PTM; AtomX uses adaptive CNA and its own layer-counting rule, so results near partial dislocations can differ.","https://docs.ovito.org/reference/pipelines/modifiers/identify_fcc_planar_faults.html"},
    {"analysis","Spatial correlation function (OVITO workflow, contributed by L. Pastewka)","C(r) = <P1(0) P2(r)> with value/difference plots and RDF/covariance normalization as described in the OVITO manual. AtomX grids property densities at the chosen spacing, correlates them by discrete Fourier transform, zero-pads non-periodic axes, normalizes by the window autocorrelation, and radially averages up to half the cell width. The direct neighbor sum is exact. OVITO's code is not used.","https://docs.ovito.org/reference/pipelines/modifiers/correlation_function.html"},
    {"formats","VASP POSCAR format","Official file-format reference; POSCAR export uses the literal default filename POSCAR.","https://www.vasp.at/wiki/index.php/POSCAR"},
    {"formats","LAMMPS data / dump formats","Official data/dump format references. AtomX's readers/writers are independent implementations.","https://docs.lammps.org/read_data.html"},
    {"software","Dear ImGui","MIT license; bundled source/license: third_party/imgui. Used for the native interface.","https://github.com/ocornut/imgui"},
    {"software","Direct3D 11","Microsoft Windows SDK graphics API, used for GPU viewport rendering.","https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/direct3d11/atoc-dx-graphics-direct3d-11"},
};
}
